# Windows Installer
FIELD TEST RESULT (v2.3.3): scope page display FIXED [YES]; install AND uninstall work correctly on BOTH
paths (all-users and per-user) [YES]. The installer is APPROVED for inclusion in current and future
releases. Two follow-ups below (3a/3b) are for the release AFTER the refactor/modularization lands.
[ ] 3a. Installer does not detect an EXISTING installation. It should check first, then offer
    upgrade (when the bundled version is newer) / repair / reinstall / remove. It must also handle the
    cross-scope case: already installed per-user but now being installed all-users (or vice versa) ->
    detect the other scope's install and uninstall it automatically first, so the machine never ends up
    with two copies. Read both HKLM and HKCU "Software\VisualStimulusKitTool" (Install_Dir + Scope) and
    compare DisplayVersion in the Uninstall key against ${VERSION}.
[ ] 3b. Make version-number propagation part of the build/release workflow and verifiable. VERSION must
    flow into: pyinstaller.spec APP_NAME, the onedir ZIP name, makensis /DVERSION, the NSIS
    VIProductVersion/DisplayVersion, and the release tag. Add a CI assertion that the built artifacts and
    the installer's registered DisplayVersion all match the VERSION file, so this cannot silently drift
    (it can't be verified from the installer exe alone).
[~] Fix NSIS installer: must register in Windows "Installed Apps" list and have a working uninstaller
    (per-user install scope). REWRITTEN 2026-07 (installer/videoannotation_installer.nsi) but NOT YET
    TESTED ON WINDOWS, so it is still NOT built by the workflow and the website button stays commented
    out (docs/index.html). What was fixed:
    - Source is now the ONEDIR build: `File /r "..\dist_onedir\Visual Stimulus Kit Tool\*.*"`, so
      installed users get the fast-starting one-folder version instead of the self-extracting onefile.
      (Previously it pointed at the stale path ..\dist\Visual Stimulus Kit Tool.exe.)
    - Registers in Windows "Installed Apps": writes DisplayName/DisplayVersion/Publisher/DisplayIcon/
      UninstallString/QuietUninstallString/InstallLocation/EstimatedSize/NoModify/NoRepair under the
      Uninstall key, in HKLM or HKCU to match the chosen scope. This was missing entirely - the reason
      the app never appeared in Add/Remove Programs.
    - $INSTDIR/scope consistency: the scope page now sets $INSTDIR and SetShellVarContext together in a
      page-leave callback. Previously SetOutPath and $INSTDIR could disagree, so files were installed in
      one place while the uninstaller and shortcuts pointed at another - the core reason uninstall broke.
    - Replaced the fragile relative jumps (StrCmp ... +5 / Goto +4) with LogicLib ${If} blocks.
    - FIELD BUG "no uninstaller was generated": root cause was the $INSTDIR/SetOutPath mismatch above -
      for an all-users install the app went to Program Files (SetOutPath) while Uninstall.exe was written
      to $INSTDIR (still the per-user directory-page default), so the install folder had no uninstaller
      and, with no Add/Remove entry either, there was no way to reach it. Now that $INSTDIR is
      authoritative, WriteUninstaller targets the same folder as the app, and the install ABORTS with a
      clear message if Uninstall.exe is not present afterwards rather than failing silently.
    - FIELD BUG "scope page text crammed together/unreadable": the radio buttons were sized in raw pixels
      (200x12) which clipped the long labels. The page is now laid out in dialog units so it scales with
      the system font, with a heading, roomier radio rows and an explanatory paragraph under each option.
      The two radios are created back-to-back (with WS_GROUP on the first) so they stay one
      mutually-exclusive group, and the descriptive labels are added afterwards into the gaps.
      Page header/subtitle text is set via the classic 1037/1038 dialog items.
    - Uninstaller: recovers the install scope from the registry (so it looks in the right Start Menu and
      hive), removes BOTH registry keys it created, and guards `RMDir /r $INSTDIR` behind a check that
      the app exe is actually there, so a wrong $INSTDIR cannot delete an unrelated folder.
    - All-users choice without admin rights now warns and falls back to per-user instead of silently
      failing to write HKLM/Program Files. RequestExecutionLevel highest.
    - Output renamed to Visual-Stimulus-Kit-Tool-<version>-setup.exe (matches the portable naming; the
      website's installer detection matches on /setup|installer/i so it still resolves).
    VERIFIED: compiles cleanly with `makensis -DVERSION=<v> installer/videoannotation_installer.nsi`
    against a stub dist_onedir bundle, with no errors/warnings, and makensis reports the Uninstall
    section is embedded. NOT verified: actual install/uninstall/Add-Remove behavior, and the appearance
    of the scope page, on a real Windows machine.
    TO RE-ENABLE: build the onedir spec first (--distpath dist_onedir), run makensis, then add the
    resulting installer/Visual-Stimulus-Kit-Tool-<version>-setup.exe back to the workflow's upload list
    and uncomment the website button.

# Recording Format / Archive Compliance
[x] Make microphone recording language-documentation-archive-compliant (IASA TC-04: uncompressed PCM WAV, 48 kHz, 24-bit).
    NOTE: vat/audio/recording.py now captures 48 kHz / 24-bit mono (paInt24 via wave module, sampwidth=3), with a graceful fallback to 16-bit at 48 kHz when the input device does not support 24-bit capture. Playback (vat/audio/playback.py) was already format-agnostic. Join/import sample rate bumped to 48 kHz for consistency. NOTE: the previous format was actually 44.1 kHz/16-bit, not 32-bit float as originally assumed.
[x] Avoid information loss in the "Join WAVs" derivative (vat/audio/joiner.py).
    NOTE: Joined output now upscales to 48 kHz / 32-bit (std_sample_width=4) instead of downconverting to 16-bit. pydub cannot emit true 24-bit (it upcasts 24-bit to 32-bit internally), and 32-bit preserves the 24-bit recordings losslessly. Verified empirically: recordings round-trip bit-exact (top 24 bits), and mixing the 24-bit-origin recordings with the 16-bit-generated click/silence does NOT clip or shift levels (pydub's 24->32 x256 and audioop's 16->32 x65536 both map to the same 32-bit full scale). Decision: the joined file is a non-compliant-but-lossless derivative; the per-item recordings remain the true-24-bit archival masters. Documented in docs/faq/index.html (#archiving).
[ ] (Optional, low priority) Emit TRUE 24-bit (pcm_s24le) for the joined file if a fully IASA-compliant single-file export is ever wanted. Requires bypassing pydub for the final write — shell to bundled ffmpeg (-c:a pcm_s24le -ar 48000) or write via the stdlib `wave` module (sampwidth=3) from correctly-scaled samples, with a round-trip test. Largely superseded by the 32-bit-lossless approach above, since the joined file is only a convenience copy for ELAN/SayMore and the archival masters are the individual per-item recordings.
[ ] (Optional) "Add audio" import/convert (vat/ui/app.py) stays 48 kHz / 16-bit. Left as-is deliberately: imported audio (often compressed WhatsApp voice notes per the remote workflow) is never better than its source, so 16-bit is sufficient. Revisit only if users routinely import high-bit-depth source audio worth preserving.
[ ] Warn on single-file export: when the user runs "Export as single WAV", warn them to KEEP the original
    individual recordings, because the combined file is not archive-compliant (it is a 32-bit lossless
    derivative for ELAN/SayMore, not an IASA-compliant 24-bit master). Wording should make clear the
    per-item recordings are the archival masters. Consider a "don't show again" checkbox.
[ ] Write BWF (Broadcast Wave Format) metadata into the ORIGINAL isolated recordings (the per-item masters) — IASA TC-04 recommends BWF (.wav with a `bext` chunk) as the preferred archival container.
    - Current: vat/audio/recording.py writes plain PCM WAV via the stdlib `wave` module (fmt + data chunks only); no `bext`. The `wave` module cannot write a bext chunk.
    - `bext` (EBU Tech 3285) fields worth populating: Description, Originator (app name/version), OriginatorReference, OriginationDate, OriginationTime, TimeReference, Version, and CodingHistory (e.g. "A=PCM,F=48000,W=24,M=mono,T=<app> <version>"). These give provenance an archive can trust.
    - Implementation options: (a) after recording, inject a `bext` chunk into the WAV by rewriting the RIFF (small pure-Python step, files are written once at stop); (b) run the bundled ffmpeg with bext metadata flags as a post step; (c) shell to FADGI/MediaArea `bwfmetaedit` if we ever bundle it. Prefer (a) or (b) to avoid a new dependency.
    - Scope: originals only — NOT the joined derivative. Keep it optional/robust: a malformed bext must never corrupt the audio; players ignore unknown chunks, so a correct bext is safe. Add a round-trip test that the PCM data is byte-identical before/after bext injection.

