# Architecture: ONE shared web UI + native wrappers (pywebview desktop & Android) — NEXT MAJOR PROJECT
# DECIDED 2026-07: NO PWA. The web layer is not browser-delivered; it is a shared UI codebase wrapped
# natively on each platform. Two targets only: pywebview (Windows/desktop) and a native Android app.
# This removes the whole class of "degrade gracefully in a browser" problems — notably that a browser
# cannot produce archive-compliant 24-bit/48 kHz PCM, which would make a PWA unfit for the core purpose.
[ ] Re-platform: replace the PySide6/Qt GUI with an HTML/CSS/JS UI shared UNCHANGED by both wrappers.
    Native code is reserved strictly for what genuinely requires it.
    Target shape:
    - ONE web UI: all screens and UX (All/Videos/Images/Review, playback controls, settings, metadata,
      i18n). This must NOT be forked per platform; a change here lands on desktop and Android with no
      porting work. Design rule: if a feature CAN be done in the web layer, it MUST be, so it is
      written once.
    - A NATIVE CAPABILITY CONTRACT with two implementations (Python on desktop, Kotlin/Java on Android).
      Everything native-only lives behind it: audio capture (48 kHz/24-bit where hardware allows), audio
      playback, filesystem + folder/file picker, WAV joining and format conversion, clipboard, and OS
      integration (open-in-external-editor, reveal-in-file-manager).
    - Desktop wrapper: pywebview over the existing Python (FolderAccessManager, AudioController,
      JoinWavsWorker, converters, resources.py ffmpeg resolution) exposed via window.pywebview.api.
    - Android wrapper: native WebView hosting the same UI; AudioRecord for PCM capture (48 kHz is fine;
      24-bit needs ENCODING_PCM_24BIT_PACKED and device support, else fall back to 16-bit exactly as the
      desktop app already does), Storage Access Framework for folders/files, MediaCodec or bundled ffmpeg
      for conversion. NEEDS ITS OWN PLAN before starting.
    native-layer.js (CONFIRMED APPROACH — build this FIRST, before porting any UI):
    - A single JS module is the ONLY thing that knows which host it is running in. It detects the host at
      load (pywebview injects window.pywebview.api; the Android WebView injects an @JavascriptInterface
      object) and exports platform-neutral functions the rest of the UI calls — e.g. Native.listMedia(),
      Native.startRecording(path), Native.readClipboardMedia(), Native.pickFolder(). No other file in the
      web UI may contain a platform conditional.
    - Make EVERY function Promise-based even where the host is synchronous. pywebview's api returns
      promises; Android's @JavascriptInterface is synchronous and must be wrapped (callback bridge for
      anything slow). A uniformly async facade means UI code never has to care which host it is on.
    - Native.capabilities() should report what the host can actually do (e.g. {record24bit,
      clipboardImages, clipboardFiles, folderPicker}) so the UI can disable/warn rather than fail at the
      point of use. This is how the Android 24-bit question and clipboard restrictions stay graceful.
    - Ship a THIRD implementation: a mock/dev backend so the web UI runs and is testable in a plain
      browser with no wrapper. Big productivity win and it makes the UI testable in CI.
    - Normalise errors into one shape so the UI never parses platform-specific messages.
[ ] CLIPBOARD — hardest single item in the migration; scope it as its own work package.
    Audited current state: the clipboard surface IS genuinely PySide6-bound and will not survive as-is —
    QGuiApplication.clipboard().setPixmap/setImage/setMimeData for writing (vat/ui/app.py ~L2348, L2353,
    L2403, L2460) and _clipboard_audio_to_tempfile / _clipboard_image_to_tempfile (~L2861, L2917) which
    take a QMimeData. BUT the valuable part is portable: what those helpers do AFTER obtaining the data
    (URL/file-path extraction, temp-file writing, format sniffing) plus all downstream conversion
    (pydub/PIL, no Qt). PRESERVE that logic; only the clipboard ACCESS needs reimplementing per platform.
    Contract should deal only in TEMP FILE PATHS + a kind, never in platform objects:
      Native.readClipboardMedia() -> {kind: 'audio'|'image'|'video'|null, path}   # host writes a temp file
      Native.writeClipboardFile(path, kind)                                       # host puts it on the clipboard
    Platform notes:
    - Windows/pywebview: pywebview has no clipboard API. Use pywin32 (win32clipboard) for CF_HDROP (file
      lists), CF_DIB (images) and audio formats. A rewrite of the access layer, not of the logic.
    - Android: ClipboardManager + ClipData; sharing files needs FileProvider content:// URIs plus
      grantUriPermission, and Android 10+ restricts background clipboard reads. Expect a reduced
      capability set — report it via Native.capabilities() rather than failing.
    - Do NOT use the browser Clipboard API (navigator.clipboard): it cannot do OS-level file/mime
      exchange and is heavily restricted. Clipboard MUST live in the native layer.
    - The WhatsApp remote workflow depends on mime-specific clipboard exchange with other apps (paste
      audio/image/video in and out). Test that end-to-end on BOTH platforms after migrating.
    Keeping the two platforms in step (plan deliberately; do not leave to discipline):
    - Version the contract; both wrappers assert they implement the current version at startup and fail
      loudly on mismatch rather than silently missing a method.
    - One contract-conformance test suite run against each implementation, so a capability added on
      desktop but not Android is caught by CI, not by a user.
    - Single shared i18n data (JSON) consumed by the web UI on both platforms.
    Migration considerations / risks:
    - Large rewrite; must preserve feature parity — Review tab logic & randomization, Export Sets/Results,
      YAML settings + export, click-separated joins, Ocenaudio integration, overwrite-confirm + auto-save
      UX, image-vs-video thumbnail handling.
    - The characterization tests in tests/ describe what the current UI does; treat them as the
      behavioural specification the web UI must reproduce, even though they cannot run against it.
    - Verify Android audio against the archival requirement before presenting it as fieldwork-capable; if
      a device cannot do 24-bit, say so in the UI.
